.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1253 PM PDT Fri Aug 28 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

Temperatures are struggling to warm to their forecasted maximum
values this afternoon. This is as abundant mid-to-high level cloud
cover from remnant moisture from former Tropical Storm Iselle moves
over the region. However, we are seeing via satellite that breaks in
this cloud cover are appearing off of the Central Coast. This may
result in enough heating to destabilize conditions across this
region. As such, we are keeping the Red Flag Warning in effect
through 3 PM this afternoon given the dry lightning potential.

The mid-to-upper level moisture is forecast to shift eastward into
the Sierra Nevada as the trough off of the Pacific Northwest coast
deepens. Thus, rain showers and the potential for thunderstorms
greatly diminishes after sunset. However, the aforementioned trough
will result in a deepening of the marine layer tonight and into the
upcoming weekend. Therefore, an increased probability of stratus near
the coast and into the adjacent valleys overnight tonight.

Slightly warmer temperatures are expected on Saturday as low clouds
that do develop overnight retreat back to the coastline by tomorrow
afternoon and give way to mostly sunny skies across the interior.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1253 PM PDT Fri Aug 28 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

A reinforcing trough deepens off of the Pacific Northwest coast by
Monday, allowing afternoon temperatures to be some 5 to 15 degrees
below seasonal averages. Beyond Monday and Tuesday, the latest
guidance indicates troughing off of the West Coast will be stronger
than previous anticipated. Therefore, less likely to see tropical
moisture from the Eastern Pacific to advect northward. However,
confidence remains low at this time. Be sure to keep up to date with
the latest forecast information from a trusted and reliable
source.

